      "claim": "The query is a small neutral N-methyl-N-phenyl pyrazolone (MW 188, logD 1.48, TPSA 26.9, no H-bond donors), structurally close to the pyrazolone edaravone and to the N-phenyl lactam pirfenidone.",
      "card_ids": ["C226", "C235", "C18"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The L1 gold record for the closest training context, pirfenidone, reports high human oral bioavailability, and L3 fasted-state exposure records show substantial Cmax for pirfenidone.",
      "card_ids": ["C18", "C102"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 edaravone records show very high intestinal permeability (Papp >10×10^-6 cm/s) and no P-gp efflux (efflux ratio <1, unchanged by verapamil), indicating no gut-wall efflux liability for the close pyrazolone core.",
      "card_ids": ["C226", "C235"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records for multiple small molecules show complete or near-complete human absorption (diazepam 97-100%, phenylbutazone rapid/complete, phenobarbital complete), consistent with favorable absorption for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C147", "C162", "C154", "C178", "C184"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records provide no direct query measurement; the closest pyrazolone analog edaravone shows moderate intrinsic clearance/glucuronidation in human liver microsomes, while phenobarbital shows no in vitro metabolism and perampanel has negligible first-pass metabolism despite extensive systemic metabolism, so no affirmative high-extraction first-pass liability is established.",
      "card_ids": ["C253", "C272", "C287", "C296", "C266", "C274"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C253", "C272", "C287", "C296", "C266", "C274"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct L6 hepatic clearance or metabolic-stability measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "No direct human absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query is present in the supplied cards.",
    "The closest L6 analog, edaravone, is not identical to the query (N-methyl vs N-H/positional differences), so transfer is analogical.",
    "HLM intrinsic-clearance values are not scaled to in vivo hepatic extraction, so they cannot affirmatively establish F < 20%.",
    "C89 remains an unresolved conflicting descriptor for pirfenidone, but it is outweighed by the L1 gold record and broader exposure/absorption/efflux evidence.",
    "Fasted-state-specific hepatic first-pass extraction for the query is not directly measured."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L6 hepatic-clearance/metabolic-stability evidence adds no direct query measurement; the closest pyrazolone analog edaravone shows moderate HLM intrinsic clearance/glucuronidation, but this is not affirmative evidence of high first-pass loss, and phenobarbital/perampanel records illustrate that metabolism alone does not imply low F. Combined with the L1 pirfenidone gold record, L3 fasted exposure, L4 absorption, and L5 permeability/efflux evidence, F >= 20% remains better supported. Prediction remains high."
}

